 $C$ is the closed polygonal path $OABO$, shown in the diagram above, traversed in the counterclockwise direction?"] [17592186180214 :question/answer-fields 17592186179159] [17592186180109 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3704/Source/Images/q-39342.png)\n\nThe path $C_{1}$ consists of a line segment from the point $O(0, 0)$ to the point $A(2, 0)$, and the path $C_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(2, 0)$ to the point $B(1, 1)$.\n\nGiven that $∫_(C_{1})F \\cdot dr = 2$, evaluate $∫_(C)F \\cdot dr$, where $F(x, y) = (x + y)i + (x - y)j$, and $C = C_{1}∪C_{2}$."] [17592186180111 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3704/Source/Images/q-39674.png)\n\nThe path $C_{1}$ consists of a section of the parabola $y = x^{2}$ from the point $O(0, 0)$ to the point $A(2, 4)$, and the path $C_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(2, 4)$ to the point $B(0, 4)$.\n\nGiven that $∫_(C_{2})F \\cdot dr =-2$, evaluate $∫_(C)F \\cdot dr$, where $F(x, y) = xi + yj$ and $C = C_{1}∪C_{2}$."] [17592186179530 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = (3x+2) \\: \\mathbf{i} - y^3 \\: \\mathbf{j}$ and let $C$ be a path in $\\mathbb R^2$. Write $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}$ in the form $\\displaystyle\\int_a^b P(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x + Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm d y$."] [17592186179530 :question/worked-solution "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = P(x,y) \\: \\mathbf{i} + Q(x,y) \\: \\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\ndefined on $C$, we have\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\int\\limits_C P(x,y) \\: \\text{d}x + Q(x,y) \\: \\text{d}y\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nP = 3x+2\n$$\n\nand $Q = -y^3$. Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)F \\cdot dr &= ∫_(C)(3x + 2)dx - y^{3}dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186179457 :question/problem "Consider the integral $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r}$, where $C$ is the closed polygonal path $FGHF$ shown above, traversed in the **clockwise** direction. Express this integral as a sum of line integrals along $C_1, C_2$, and $C_3$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3704/Source/Images/4c07015f012adfb9c576718541a44fe8.png)"] [17592186179457 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can represent $C$, which is traversed in clockwise, as the union of $3$ paths:\n\n$$\nC = \\{-C_1\\} \\cup \\{-C_2\\} \\cup C_3\n$$\n\nwhere $-C_1$ and $-C_2$ are the same paths as $C_1$ and $C_2$ but traversed in the opposite direction.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)F \\cdot dr &= ∫_(-C_{1})F \\cdot dr + ∫_(-C_{2})F \\cdot dr + ∫_(C_{3})F \\cdot dr \\\\\n&= - ∫_(C_{1})F \\cdot dr - ∫_(C_{2})F \\cdot dr + ∫_(C_{3})F \\cdot dr\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186179454 :question/problem "The path $C_1$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(-2,0)$ to the point $O(0,0)$, and the path $C_2$ consists of a line segment from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $B(1,1)$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3704/Source/Images/962f5b7322eaec11b61a09faa7e19175.png)\n\nGiven that $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = -4$, evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r}$, where $\\mathbf{F}(x, y) = (2x-3y) \\,\\mathbf{i} + (2y-5x)\\,\\mathbf {j}$ and $C = C_1\\cup C_2$."] [17592186179454 :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} + \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r}\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the first integral, so we only need to work out the second.\n\nThe path $C_2$ is the line segment from $O$ to $B$. It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_{2}(t) &= b + t(c - b) \\\\\n&= ⟨0, 0⟩ + t(⟨1, 1⟩ - ⟨0, 0⟩) \\\\\n&= ⟨t, t⟩ \\\\\n&= ti + tj\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0 \\lt t \\lt 1$. Along this segment, we have $x = t$ and $y = t$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(r_{2}(t)) &= (2x - 3y)i + (2y - 5x)j \\\\\n&= (2t - 3t)i + (2t - 5t)j \\\\\n&=-ti - 3tj\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_{2}^{′}(t) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(t)j \\\\\n&= i + j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(r_{2}(t)) \\cdot r_{2}^{′}(t) &= (-ti - 3tj) \\cdot (i + j) \\\\\n&=-t \\cdot 1 + (-3t) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&=-4t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)F \\cdot dr &= ∫_(C_{1})F \\cdot dr + ∫_(C_{2})F \\cdot dr \\\\\n&=-4 + ∫_{0}^{1}F(r_{2}(t)) \\cdot r_{2}^{′}(t)dt \\\\\n&=-4 + ∫_{0}^{1}(-4t)dt \\\\\n&=-4 + [- 2t^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&=-4 + [- 2 + 0] \\\\\n&=-6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186177695 17592186177696 17592186177697 17592186177698 17592186177699 17592186178530 17592186178531 17592186178532 17592186178533 17592186178534 17592186178542 17592186178543 17592186178544 17592186178545 17592186178546 17592186179160 17592186179161 17592186179162 17592186179163 17592186179164] "immutable_fields" [[17592186177694 :answer-field/correct] [17592186177694 :answer-field/id] [17592186177694 :answer-field/key] [17592186177694 :answer-field/type] [17592186178529 :answer-field/correct] [17592186178529 :answer-field/id] [17592186178529 :answer-field/key] [17592186178529 :answer-field/type] [17592186178541 :answer-field/correct] [17592186178541 :answer-field/id] [17592186178541 :answer-field/key] [17592186178541 :answer-field/type] [17592186179159 :answer-field/correct] [17592186179159 :answer-field/id] [17592186179159 :answer-field/key] [17592186179159 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
